    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "c112e284_4639501b",
        "filename": "kernel/sched/fair.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 6272,
      "author": {
        "id": 1119068
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-06-15T11:17:37Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Let\u0027s try to compare old with new code step-by-step.\n\nFor prefer_idle tasks, in the old code we just went for the first idle CPU we find. Code was:\n\n    if (idle_cpu(i) \u0026\u0026 prefer_idle) {\n        schedstat_inc(p, se.statistics.nr_wakeups_fbt_pref_idle);\n        schedstat_inc(this_rq(), eas_stats.fbt_pref_idle);\n        return i;\n    }\n\nand this was the really first block in the original do{}while() loop (line 6259). In the new code I\u0027ve just moved the check on idle_cpu(i) inside what I called \"case A\".\n\nIf there are not IDLE CPUs, then you say we should still pick the CPU with the highest capacity_curr_of. This case (prefer_idle task on non idle CPUs) was covered in lines 6273-6281:\n\n    if (prefer_idle) {\n        /* Favor the CPU that last ran the task */\n        if (new_util \u003e target_util ||\n            wake_util \u003e min_wake_util)\n            continue;\n        min_wake_util \u003d wake_util;\n        target_util \u003d new_util;\n        target_cpu \u003d i;\n    }\n\nWhich is what the new code is doing in the remaining of \"case A\", lines 6281-6296.\n\nRemaining bits of the original code are:\n\n    cur_capacity \u003d capacity_curr_of(i);\n    if (new_util \u003c cur_capacity) {\n      // This case is covered because of previous explanations\n      // thus we are not looking at the next branch:\n    } else if (backup_capacity \u003e cur_capacity) {\n        /* Find a backup cpu with least capacity. */\n        backup_capacity \u003d cur_capacity;\n        backup_cpu \u003d i;\n    }\n\nSo, under all these conditions:\na) current CPU not IDLE\nb) new_util \u003e\u003d cur_capacity \u003d\u003d capacity_curr_of(i); // to skip the first branch\nc) backup_capacity \u003e cur_capacity // to get the second branch\n\nthen for all tasks (prefer_idle or not) we keep track of the current CPU as a backup.\n\nDoes that not means that the backup CPU for a prefer_idle task, if we do not get a chance to find an idle CPU before, is just the one with the lower capacity in the system (i.e. a LITTLE CPU)?\nThis match also with what the original comment says.\n\nAccording to this analysis, I cannot find a path where we used to favour higher OPP non-idle CPUs for idle task... but the original code is so convoluted which maybe I\u0027ve missed a code-path.\n\nIn any case I would say that favouring higher capacity_curr_of() should be better for prefer_idle tasks. But if we cannot find it in the old code we should probably add it on top as a separate improvement patch.\nNotice that in the new code structure we know exactly that what we do for prefer_idle tasks is completely defined within the \"case A\" block, thus it should be easy to add such an improvement.",
      "parentUuid": "2da4a534_ce8f2a19",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 6272,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 6272,
        "endChar": 21
      },
      "revId": "f568a05cdb2a58e878634a200827ea06eb1289df",
