      "writtenOn": "2017-06-15T11:17:37Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This is just a break-down of the above comment:\n\n    Keep track of CPU with smallest capacity and highest \n    utilization (task packing).\n\nBut you are right, maybe it can be better described using something like:\n\nA task packing strategy requires to put as many tasks as possible in a given capacity. Thus, assuming two CPUs have the same capacity, a task should be put in the one of the two CPUs where:\n a) it fits in its current capacity\n b) it uses most of its spare capacity\nIndeed, in task packing we do not want to increase the current OPP (a) while, at the same time, a reducing the spare capacity (b) allows to maximize the compute efficiency.\n\nThis last point is however a bit sensible for boosted tasks.\nIn this case indeed we can potentially add an optimization patch on top where we go for the CPU with maximum spare capacity in the attempt to reduce the risk of performance penalties on the task due to co-scheduling latencies.\n\nI\u0027ll update the comment an possible add this optimization patch on top.",
      "parentUuid": "750c5895_f9c066d3",
