    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "101a3aae_3ad97266",
        "filename": "kernel/sched/fair.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 6377,
      "author": {
        "id": 1119068
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-06-15T11:17:37Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "As I reported in the comment of \"case B\" for non latency sensitive tasks:\n\n   If idle CPUs are available, favour these CPUs to improve \n   performances by spreading tasks. Indeed, the energy_diff() \n   computed by the caller will take care to ensure the \n   minimization of energy consumption without affecting \n   performance.\n\nThe point here is that we can try to pick a more spreading strategy to favour performance for these tasks as well and then be assured that the energy_diff will filter out all placements which are estimated to be to aggressive in energy.\n\nBut I reckon this is a variation wrt the previous code and thus I should probably better split it out on a different patch for better testing. Moreover, this optimization should be probably reserved to boosted tasks, while we can go for a more strict packing strategy for !boosted and !prefer_idle tasks.",
